      "text": "To solve the equation \\((x - 5)^2 = 9\\), we start by taking the square root of both sides. The square root of a squared term gives us the absolute value of the original term, so we have:\n\n\\[\n|x - 5| = 3\n\\]\n\nThis absolute value equation can be split into two separate equations:\n\n\\[\nx - 5 = 3 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad x - 5 = -3\n\\]\n\nWe solve each equation separately. For the first equation:\n\n\\[\nx - 5 = 3\n\\]\nAdding 5 to both sides, we get:\n\\[\nx = 8\n\\]\n\nFor the second equation:\n\n\\[\nx - 5 = -3\n\\]\nAdding 5 to both sides, we get:\n\\[\nx = 2\n\\]\n\nThe roots of the equation are \\(x = 8\\) and \\(x = 2\\). To find the sum of the roots, we add these solutions together:\n\n\\[\n8 + 2 = 10\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the sum of the roots is \\(\\boxed{10}\\).",
