    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To find the sum of the roots of the equation \\((x - 5)^2 = 9\\), we first need to solve the equation for \\(x\\).\n\n1. Start by isolating the term inside the square. We do this by taking the square root of both sides of the equation:\n\\[\n(x - 5)^2 = 9\n\\]\n\\[\n\\sqrt{(x - 5)^2} = \\sqrt{9}\n\\]\nSince the square root of a square returns the absolute value, we have:\n\\[\n|x - 5| = 3\n\\]\nThis absolute value equation gives us two possible cases to consider:\n\\[\nx - 5 = 3 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad x - 5 = -3\n\\]\n\n2. Solve each equation separately:\n\\[\nx - 5 = 3 \\implies x = 3 + 5 \\implies x = 8\n\\]\n\\[\nx - 5 = -3 \\implies x = -3 + 5 \\implies x = 2\n\\]\n\nSo, the solutions to the equation \\((x - 5)^2 = 9\\) are \\(x = 8\\) and \\(x = 2\\).\n\n3. The roots of the equation are \\(x = 8\\) and \\(x = 2\\). To find the sum of the roots, we add these values together:\n\\[\n8 + 2 = 10\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the sum of the roots is \\(\\boxed{10}\\).",
